WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
You already know the accounting equation: assets equal liabilities plus equity. Every ratio in this chapter is that equation rearranged, which is why they are all deducible from one another and why a question that gives you one can always be answered for the others.
9.1  The four ratios
Each of the four divides a measure of debt by a measure of what supports it.
The debt-to-equity ratio is total liabilities divided by total stockholders' equity. It asks how many dollars of other people's money the company carries for each dollar of the owners'.
The long-term debt-to-equity ratio uses only long-term debt in the numerator. It removes the trade payables and accruals that arise from trading rather than from a financing decision, so it isolates the borrowing the board chose.
The debt-to-total-assets ratio is total liabilities divided by total assets. It asks what share of everything the company owns is financed by creditors.
The equity multiplier, which the Learning Outcome Statements also call the financial leverage ratio, is total assets divided by total stockholders' equity. It asks how many dollars of assets each dollar of equity supports.
Figure F509-02 computes all four for Orontes.
Figure F509-02. Orontes Foods, Inc. — the capital structure ratios
@table
Ratio | Formula | 2025 | 2024 | Movement
Debt to equity | Total liabilities ÷ total equity | 0.55 | 0.61 | Fell
Long-term debt to equity | Long-term debt ÷ total equity | 0.29 | 0.34 | Fell
Debt to total assets | Total liabilities ÷ total assets | 0.36 | 0.38 | Fell
Equity multiplier | Total assets ÷ total equity | 1.55 | 1.61 | Fell
@end
The arithmetic for 2025. Debt to equity is 11,279,625 ÷ 20,392,975 = 0.55. Long-term debt to equity is 6,000,000 ÷ 20,392,975 = 0.29. Debt to total assets is 11,279,625 ÷ 31,672,600 = 0.36. The equity multiplier is 31,672,600 ÷ 20,392,975 = 1.55.
All four fell, and they fell for one reason: equity grew 24.5 per cent while liabilities grew 12.0 per cent. Orontes financed its growth mainly out of retained profit and a share issue. The capital structure is conservative and became more so.
EXAM TRAP   Which debt is in the numerator?
Debt to equity normally uses TOTAL liabilities, including payables and accruals.
Long-term debt to equity uses only the long-term borrowing. Whether the current portion is included varies; the exam states which it wants.
Debt to total assets and the equity multiplier both use total liabilities, one against assets and one through equity.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the four ratios are one fact
Step 1. Start from debt to total assets, 0.36. That means equity to total assets is 1.00 − 0.36 = 0.64.
Step 2. Debt to equity follows. 0.36 ÷ 0.64 = 0.56, which is the 0.55 of the figure, the difference being rounding.
Step 3. The equity multiplier follows too. 1.00 ÷ 0.64 = 1.56, again the 1.55 of the figure within rounding.
Check. Three ratios, one underlying split of 36 per cent debt and 64 per cent equity. If an exam question gives you any one of the three, you can produce the other two without further information, and that is a question the exam sets.
