Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
9.2  What a change in the composition does
The syllabus asks for the effect on capital structure and solvency of a change in the composition of debt against equity, and the method is to restate the balance sheet and recompute. Three transactions cover most of what the exam asks.
Borrowing to buy an asset raises liabilities and assets by the same amount and leaves equity alone. Every one of the four ratios rises.
Borrowing to buy back shares raises liabilities, reduces equity, and leaves total assets roughly unchanged. The ratios rise much more sharply, because the numerator grows while the denominator shrinks.
Issuing shares to repay debt does the reverse of the second: liabilities fall, equity rises, assets are unchanged. All four ratios fall.
Figure F509-03 works the three through Orontes's 2025 balance sheet.
Figure F509-03. Three financing decisions, from Orontes's 2025 position
@table
Decision | Total liabilities | Total equity | Total assets | Debt to equity | Equity multiplier
As reported | 11,279,625 | 20,392,975 | 31,672,600 | 0.55 | 1.55
Borrow 8,000,000 to build a plant | 19,279,625 | 20,392,975 | 39,672,600 | 0.95 | 1.95
Borrow 8,000,000 to buy back shares | 19,279,625 | 12,392,975 | 31,672,600 | 1.56 | 2.56
Issue 8,000,000 of shares to repay debt | 3,279,625 | 28,392,975 | 31,672,600 | 0.12 | 1.12
@end
The second row is the one to understand. The same $8,000,000 of borrowing takes debt to equity from 0.55 to 0.95 when it buys an asset, and to 1.56 when it buys back shares — nearly twice as far — because a buy-back removes equity from the denominator at the same time as it adds debt to the numerator. Boards do this deliberately, and a lender reading the statements afterwards sees a different company.
Note what does not change in the second row. The business is the same: the same plant, the same customers, the same operating income. What changed is who has a claim on it and in what order. That is the whole of a capital structure decision, and Book 2 takes it up as a decision rather than a measurement.
EXAM TRAP   Buying an asset is not the same as buying back shares
Borrowing to invest raises both halves of the balance sheet.
Borrowing to distribute raises liabilities and reduces equity, so the ratios move about twice as far.
Retained earnings raise equity without any transaction at all, which is why a profitable company deleverages by standing still.
