9.3  The risk profile
A capital structure decision changes three things about a company, and the exam asks about all three.
It changes who bears the loss first. Equity absorbs losses before debt does. A company financed 90 per cent by equity can lose a great deal before a lender is at risk; a company financed 90 per cent by debt cannot.
It changes the fixed charge. Interest must be paid in a bad year. Dividends need not: the board can simply not declare one, as Orontes could have chosen in 2025. That asymmetry is the heart of financial risk.
It changes the return to the owners when things go well. Borrowing at 6 per cent to earn 12 per cent leaves the difference for the shareholders, so leverage raises return on equity as long as the company earns more than its interest. Chapter 14 measures that directly through the DuPont decomposition.
Figure F509-04 sets the trade-off out.
Figure F509-04. What more debt does
@table
| More debt | More equity
Fixed charge | Rises; must be paid in a bad year | None; dividends are discretionary
Return to owners in a good year | Higher | Lower
Loss borne by owners in a bad year | Larger, proportionately | Smaller
Control | Unchanged | Diluted among more shareholders
Tax | Interest is deductible | Dividends are not
Who is at risk first | Equity, then debt | Equity, and there is more of it
@end
The tax row is the one that makes the decision genuinely difficult. Interest reduces taxable income and dividends do not, so debt is cheaper after tax than its interest rate suggests. Orontes pays interest of $390,000; at its effective tax rate of about 25 per cent, the after-tax cost is roughly 390,000 × 0.75 = 292,500. Book 2 develops this as the cost of capital.
Orontes's own risk profile is the most conservative in this book. Debt is 36 per cent of assets, the equity multiplier is 1.55, and Chapter 10 will show interest covered 11.15 times. The board has room to borrow and has not used it. Whether that is prudence or timidity is the question Chapter 14 returns to, because an unlevered balance sheet also holds the return on equity down.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: what the unused capacity is worth
Step 1. Suppose Orontes borrowed enough to take debt to total assets to 0.50, the level many food manufacturers carry. Total assets would have to be financed half by debt.
Step 2. On the present balance sheet that means liabilities of 31,672,600 × 0.50 = 15,836,300, against the present 11,279,625. The additional borrowing is 15,836,300 − 11,279,625 = 4,556,675.
Step 3. At 6 per cent the extra interest is about 4,556,675 × 0.06 = 273,401, against earnings before interest and taxes of $4,348,800. Interest cover would fall from 11.15 times to about 4,348,800 ÷ 663,401 = 6.56 times, which is still strong.
Check. Orontes could raise over four and a half million dollars without taking its interest cover below six and a half times. That is what a conservative capital structure buys: not safety for its own sake, but the ability to act when something is worth acting on.
