9.4  Covenants and the composition of the debt
A leverage ratio is not only an analyst's measure. It is often a term of the loan, and that gives it consequences.
A covenant is a promise in a loan agreement, and the commonest are expressed as leverage and coverage ratios: debt to equity not above a stated figure, interest cover not below one. Part 1 recorded exactly this for Barada: at December 31 it had broken a debt-to-equity covenant on its note, and the bank waived the breach on January 20 before the statements were issued. You learned there what the waiver did to the classification; here the point is what the covenant does to the company.
A breach usually makes the loan repayable on demand. That turns long-term debt into a current liability and can wreck a liquidity position overnight, which is why a company close to a covenant limit manages towards it. The measures of Chapter 7 are used for exactly this purpose, and so is the timing of a share issue.
Figure F509-05 lists what an analyst looks for.
Figure F509-05. Reading the debt, not just the ratio
@table
What to look for | Where it is disclosed | Why it matters
Covenant ratios and their limits | The borrowings note | A breach can make the debt current
Maturity profile | The borrowings note | Debt all maturing in one year is a different risk from debt spread over ten
Fixed or floating rate | The borrowings note | Floating-rate debt moves the fixed charge with interest rates
Security given | The borrowings note | Secured lenders rank ahead; unsecured creditors see less
Guarantees and off-balance-sheet obligations | Commitments and contingencies | Obligations the ratio does not count
@end
Orontes's own note, as Part 1 recorded, carries $6,000,000 of long-term debt and $400,000 of it due within the year. The maturity profile is gentle and the cover is wide. Barada's is neither, which is the difference between the two companies that the ratios alone understate.
EXAM TRAP   The ratio and the agreement
A covenant may define debt or equity differently from the published ratio. Read the definition in the agreement.
A waiver obtained after the reporting date does not change the classification under IFRS, and does under U.S. GAAP where the agreement is in place before the statements are issued.
Off-balance-sheet obligations are a real claim and no ratio in this chapter counts them.
LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Covenant"
In ordinary English a covenant is a solemn promise. In a loan agreement it is a specific undertaking, and the exam uses it only in that sense. Arabic uses التعهدات المالية and French clauses restrictives or covenants. Note the pair of adjectives the exam uses: an affirmative covenant requires the borrower to do something, and a negative covenant forbids something.
