Chapter summary
Figure F509-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F509-07. Chapter 9 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Four ratios describe one fact. Debt to equity is total liabilities over equity, 0.55 for Orontes in 2025; long-term debt to equity isolates the borrowing the board chose, 0.29; debt to total assets is 0.36; and the equity multiplier, which the syllabus also calls the financial leverage ratio, is total assets over equity, 1.55. All four follow from the split of 36 per cent debt and 64 per cent equity, so any one gives the others. All four fell during 2025 because equity grew 24.5 per cent against liabilities at 12.0. A change in composition is measured by restating and recomputing: borrowing to buy an asset raises both halves of the balance sheet, while borrowing to repurchase shares raises debt and reduces equity and so moves the ratios about twice as far. More debt means a larger fixed charge, a larger loss to the owners in a bad year, a higher return in a good one, and a deductible cost that equity does not have. Covenants turn these ratios into terms of the loan, and a breach can make long-term debt current overnight.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
