Case-style practice: two offers for Barada
Barada Wholesale needs $1,500,000 to fund its seasonal stock. Levant Commerce Bank offers a term loan at 7 per cent with a covenant limiting debt to equity to 1.50. A private investor offers to subscribe $1,500,000 of new shares for a quarter of the company. Barada's total liabilities are $3,900,000, its total equity is $3,100,000 and its total assets are $7,000,000.
Required. Compute the debt-to-equity ratio and the equity multiplier under each offer. Say whether the loan would breach the covenant. Then name one advantage and one disadvantage of each offer from the point of view of Barada's existing owner. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC9-1  Answer B. The equity multiplier is total assets over total equity.
A is wrong. That is the debt-to-equity ratio.
C is wrong. That is the equity ratio, the reciprocal of the multiplier.
D is wrong. This compares assets with liabilities, which is not a named ratio here.
SC9-2  Answer B. If debt is 0.40 of assets, equity is 0.60, and 1.00 ÷ 0.60 = 1.67.
A is wrong. 0.60 is equity to assets.
C is wrong. This would follow from debt at 0.60 of assets.
D is wrong. This is debt to equity, 0.40 ÷ 0.60.
SC9-3  Answer B. It removes payables and accruals, which arise from trading.
A is wrong. Both use carrying amounts.
C is wrong. Equity is the same denominator in both.
D is wrong. Neither ratio is computed after tax.
SC9-4  Answer A. Assets and liabilities rise while equity is unchanged, so assets over equity rises.
B is wrong. The numerator grows and the denominator does not.
C is wrong. The numerator changes.
D is wrong. Profitability does not affect this transaction.
SC9-5  Answer B. Debt rises and equity falls, so the numerator and denominator move in opposite directions.
A is wrong. The rate is not part of the ratio.
C is wrong. Assets are roughly unchanged in a buy-back.
D is wrong. Tax does not drive the ratio movement.
SC9-6  Answer B. Retained earnings raise equity with no change in liabilities.
A is wrong. Equity grows, so the ratio falls.
C is wrong. Equity changes.
D is wrong. The direction is determined.
SC9-7  Answer B. Interest is contractual; a dividend is declared at the board's discretion.
A is wrong. Size is not the asymmetry.
C is wrong. Dividends are not deductible.
D is wrong. Interest is paid to lenders.
SC9-8  Answer B. Deductible interest reduces the tax bill, so the net cost is lower than the rate.
A is wrong. Lenders do accept lower returns, but that is a different point from the tax effect.
C is wrong. Principal repayment is not an expense.
D is wrong. Security affects the rate, not the deductibility.
SC9-9  Answer B. A low multiplier means assets are financed mostly by equity.
A is wrong. Low leverage means capacity remains.
C is wrong. Low leverage reduces insolvency risk.
D is wrong. Dividend policy is a separate matter.
SC9-10  Answer B. Breach typically accelerates the debt, making it repayable on demand.
A is wrong. A breach does not improve the terms.
C is wrong. A breach releases the lender rather than binding it.
D is wrong. Forgiveness is not a consequence of breach.
SC9-11  Answer B. A floating rate moves the interest charge with market rates.
A is wrong. Maturity concerns when the principal falls due.
C is wrong. Security concerns ranking on default.
D is wrong. Covenants are limits, not pricing.
SC9-12  Answer B. Level C requires direction, magnitude and the reader it matters to.
A is wrong. Direction alone is Level B.
C is wrong. The journal entry belongs to Part 1.
D is wrong. Tax is one element of the answer, not the answer.
